// THE PUBLICATION CREDENTIAL BOUNDARY IS A UNIX PRINCIPAL, NOT A TRANSPORT.
//
// gunbc.live_deploy.spec deployment_credential_env_path records why the obvious repairs were
// withdrawn, and both of its arguments are about WHO can read, never about HOW the token is
// carried. A service-wide environment source reaches the whole process AND every child, and the
// belt spawns providers through `env -C <workdir> <program>` with neither -i nor -u, so a token
// bound into the belt unit reaches git, tmux and the dispatched provider session -- where `gh`
// consults GH_TOKEN then GITHUB_TOKEN and OVERRIDES the identity that worker was meant to act as.
// Moving the token from a variable to a file stops the automatic inheritance and creates no
// authorization boundary at all, because both units run as the deployment service user and the
// worker is spawned beneath that process.
//
// SO THE BOUNDARY IS THE PRINCIPAL. This module is the whole of what runs under it: a request
// carrying a publication SUBJECT is handed across, and a receipt is handed back. The property the
// boundary rests on is that NO PROCESS HOLDING THIS TOKEN SPAWNS A PROVIDER, TMUX OR WORKER
// SESSION -- stated that way rather than as `no children`, because the weaker sentence is the one
// that is true and it is still sufficient. This process does spawn children: `printenv` for the
// presence probe and `git` for the ref advertisement. Neither is a session anything is dispatched
// into, and neither survives the run. gunbc.fleet_posix_accounts
// fleet_posix_publication_helper_sudo_grants states the other half -- the principal holds no sudo
// grant of any shape, so it cannot act as anybody else either.
//
// THE REF ADVERTISEMENT NEEDS NO CREDENTIAL, and that is a modeled fact rather than an observed
// convenience: gunbc.repository gunbc_repository carries `private: false`, so `git ls-remote` over
// HTTPS is anonymous. It is worth stating because the tempting reading of this boundary is that the
// helper's environment must reproduce every credential the belt had, and the measurement says
// otherwise -- an over-broad credential request would be the same class of defect this module
// exists to remove. If that row ever becomes `private: true` this sentence stops being true, which
// is why it is cited to the row rather than to a run.
//
// WHY THE CREDENTIAL SOURCE IS STILL AN ENVIRONMENT VARIABLE, which looks like the very thing that
// was withdrawn and is not. The withdrawal was of a variable bound into a unit WHOSE CHILDREN ARE
// THE LEAK, and this unit has none; the transport was never the defect. A file-backed
// std.credentials.CredentialSource variant was considered and is not built here, for a reason worth
// recording rather than rediscovering: the v1 seed's REST realization resolves svc_auth_source by
// reading a `name` field as an environment variable and nothing else, so a file variant is a change
// to the frozen seed's interpreter that does not serve the v2 self-host program and would therefore
// need its own operator ruling. The variant would buy no boundary this principal does not already
// provide.
//
// THE REQUEST IS A DOCUMENT AND NOT A CALL, and that is forced rather than chosen. The service user
// holds no sudo grant and no systemctl grant, so it cannot start a unit under another principal;
// the only verb it has across the boundary is creating a file in a directory the helper owns. A
// timer drives the helper on the same cadence as the belt, so a request is answered within one
// period and every state in between is a named, recorded one.
//
// WHAT IS NOT CLAIMED. This does not model a WORKER credential and does not strip GH_TOKEN or
// GITHUB_TOKEN at the provider spawn. That ordering was ruled explicitly: with no worker source
// modeled or observed, removing those variables could take away the only authentication a worker
// currently has, trading a scope defect for a capability loss.
// THE SPOOL KEY IS A DIGEST OF THE COMPLETE SUBJECT, and the earlier revision of this function is
// the reason the rule is stated rather than assumed. It keyed on node id, attempt key and head, and
// the annotation here asserted those were "the three fields that make a publication judgment
// unique". They are not. RoadmapPublicationSubject carries SIX fields -- repository, branch and
// expected_base as well -- so two different publication questions could address the same request
// and answer path. That is keying an address on less than its declared inputs, the same shape as a
// cache key that omits half of what it depends on, and DESIGN section 5 names it directly: the
// declaration can be edited green while the realization still collides.
//
// THE COLLISION`S TWO HALVES HAVE DIFFERENT SEVERITIES, and only one of them was already contained.
// On the ANSWER side a collision could not have produced a wrong answer, because
// publication_answer_read_at compares all six fields through gunbc.roadmap_publish
// publication_receipt_subject_match and refuses a mismatched document as AnswerUnreadable rather
// than reading it as this subject`s answer. On the REQUEST side nothing compared anything: two
// subjects sharing a key write to one path, so the second silently overwrites the first, the helper
// answers whichever document it finds, and the losing subject`s ask disappears with no diagnostic.
// The belt re-asks every tick, so neither subject converges and the failure renders as a lamp that
// never settles -- a liveness defect with no located cause, which is the worst reading to debug.
//
// SO THE KEY IS THE DIGEST OF THE SERIALIZED SUBJECT, which is the one value that changes whenever
// any field does. It is derived from publication_subject_json -- the SAME serialization the request
// document carries -- so the key cannot drift away from the subject it names by someone adding a
// field to one and not the other.
//
// AND THIS RETIRES A VALIDATOR RATHER THAN ADDING ONE (DESIGN section 4b, construction over
// validation). The previous key interpolated ledger-supplied strings into a filename, so a value
// carrying `/` or `..` would have addressed a file outside the spool, and a PublicationSpoolAddress
// coproduct refused that case. A lower-hex-16 digest cannot carry a path separator: the invalid
// state has no representation instead of being caught, so the refusal arm and its witnesses are
// deleted rather than kept -- a check whose RED is no longer authorable is a decoration that would
// be cited as coverage.
//
// THE CAST IS TOTAL BY CONSTRUCTION, not by inspection: the hashed text begins with the schema
// literal, so the joined string is non-empty whatever the subject serializes to.
fn publication_spool_key(subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject) -> String {
  content_hash_atom(
    value: join([
      publication_request_schema as String,
      "|",
      serialize_json(v: publication_subject_json(subject: subject)),
    ], "") as NonEmptyStr,
  ).digest as String
}

fn publication_spool_entry_name(key: String) -> String {
  join([key, ".json"], "")
}

fn publication_request_path_for(instance_root: String, key: String) -> String {
  join([
    publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root),
    "/",
    publication_spool_entry_name(key: key),
  ], "")
}

fn publication_answer_path_for(instance_root: String, key: String) -> String {
  join([
    publication_answer_dir(instance_root: instance_root),
    "/",
    publication_spool_entry_name(key: key),
  ], "")
}

// THE REQUEST CARRIES THE SUBJECT AND NOTHING ELSE, AND CARRIES NO DESTINATION.
//
// The tempting shape is a request that names the path its answer should be written to, because the
// belt already knows that path and the helper would then need no addressing of its own. It is the
// one field that must never exist: the requester sits on the far side of a privilege boundary, so a
// caller-supplied destination is a caller-supplied write position for a principal the caller cannot
// otherwise reach. The helper derives the answer address from the subject by the same function the
// belt used to derive the request address, so the two agree by construction and neither is stated.
//
// THE SCHEMA IS A WALL RATHER THAN A HINT, on the precedent publication_receipt_schema_v2_note sets
// in gunbc.roadmap_publish: a document whose schema is not exactly this string is refused, so a
// future request shape cannot be silently read as this one with members missing.
data publication_request_schema: NonEmptyStr = "roadmap-publication-request/v2"

fn publication_request_json(subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject, worktree: String) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: publication_request_schema as String)),
    json_kv(key: "subject", value: publication_subject_json(subject: subject)),
    json_kv(key: "worktree", value: json_string(s: worktree)),
  ]))
}

type PublicationRequestDecode
  = RequestDecoded { subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject, worktree: String }
  | RequestUndecodable { reason: String }

fn publication_request_decode_document(doc: JsonValue) -> PublicationRequestDecode {
  match publication_receipt_member(doc: doc, key: "schema") {
    ReceiptMemberUnreadable { reason: r } => RequestUndecodable { reason: r }
    ReceiptMemberRead { value: schema } =>
      if schema != (publication_request_schema as String) {
        RequestUndecodable {
          reason: join([
            "publication request schema is ", schema,
            ", not ", publication_request_schema as String,
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        match json_object_unique_member(v: doc, key: "subject") {
          JsonMemberFound { value: subject_doc } =>
            match publication_subject_decode(doc: subject_doc) {
              SubjectUndecodable { reason: r } => RequestUndecodable { reason: r }
              SubjectDecoded { subject } =>
                match publication_receipt_member(doc: doc, key: "worktree") {
                  ReceiptMemberUnreadable { reason: r } => RequestUndecodable { reason: r }
                  ReceiptMemberRead { value: worktree } => RequestDecoded { subject: subject, worktree: worktree }
                }
            }
          _ =>
            RequestUndecodable {
              reason: "publication request subject member is absent or duplicated",
            }
        }
      }
  }
}

fn publication_request_decode(request: String) -> PublicationRequestDecode {
  match parse_json_document(s: request) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: gap } =>
      RequestUndecodable {
        reason: concat("the publication request is ", json_document_gap_text(gap: gap)),
      }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: doc } => publication_request_decode_document(doc: doc)
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE REQUESTING SIDE -- what the belt and the serve route do, holding no credential.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// ABSENCE IS ESTABLISHED BY A SUCCESSFUL LISTING, NEVER BY A FAILED READ, which is the same rule
// gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate publication_receipt_absence_is_established_note states for the
// attempt's own receipt and for the same reason: Filesystem.Read answers success=false both for a
// file that does not exist and for one that exists and cannot be read, and those two worlds have
// opposite correct actions -- ask again, versus stop and say what broke.
//
// A DECODABLE ANSWER ABOUT ANOTHER SUBJECT IS NOT AN ANSWER, and it is refused rather than being
// treated as absent. The answer's address is derived from the subject, so a mismatch means
// something wrote a receipt for a different judgment into this judgment's slot -- unexplained
// state, and the belt's move on unexplained state is to stop. Treating it as absent would enqueue a
// second request whose answer would land at the same address, which is a silent overwrite of the
// only evidence that it happened.
type PublicationAnswerRead
  = AnswerAbsent
  | AnswerPresent { receipt: PublicationReceipt }
  | AnswerUnreadable { reason: String }

fn publication_answer_read_at(path: String, expected: RoadmapPublicationSubject) -> PublicationAnswerRead {
  let read = Filesystem.Read(path: path)
  if !read.success {
    AnswerUnreadable {
      reason: join([
        "a publication answer for this head was listed and then could not be read, so it exists and its content is unavailable: ",
        path, " (", read.error, ")",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    match publication_receipt_decode(receipt: read.content) {
      ReceiptUndecodable { reason: r } =>
        AnswerUnreadable {
          reason: join([
            "the publication answer at ", path, " does not decode: ", r,
          ], ""),
        }
      ReceiptDecoded { receipt } =>
        match publication_receipt_subject_match(expected: expected, found: receipt.subject) {
          SubjectMatches => AnswerPresent { receipt: receipt }
          SubjectDiffers { field, expected: e, found: f } =>
            AnswerUnreadable {
              reason: join([
                "the publication answer at this subject's own address is about another subject (",
                field as String, " expected ", e, ", found ", f,
                "), which is unexplained state rather than a missing answer",
              ], ""),
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

fn publication_answer_read(
  instance_root: String,
  key: String,
  expected: RoadmapPublicationSubject,
) -> PublicationAnswerRead {
  let dir = publication_answer_dir(instance_root: instance_root)
  let listing = Filesystem.List(path: dir)
  if !listing.success {
    AnswerUnreadable {
      reason: join([
        "the publication answer directory could not be listed, so whether an answer exists was not established: ",
        dir, " (", listing.error, ") — the publication helper's spool is created by the deploy and owned by its own principal",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else if !filesystem_listing_names_entry(
    listing: listing.entries,
    name: publication_spool_entry_name(key: key),
  ) {
    AnswerAbsent
  } else {
    publication_answer_read_at(
      path: publication_answer_path_for(instance_root: instance_root, key: key),
      expected: expected,
    )
  }
}

// THE ENQUEUE IS IDEMPOTENT BY ADDRESS, not by a check. The request's name is the subject's own
// identity, and its content is a pure function of that subject, so writing it twice writes the same
// bytes to the same path -- there is no `already queued` state to observe and get wrong, and a
// re-enqueue while the helper is mid-answer costs one identical write.
type PublicationRequestEnqueue
  = RequestEnqueued { path: String }
  | RequestNotEnqueued { detail: String }

fn publication_request_enqueue(
  instance_root: String,
  key: String,
  subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject,
  worktree: String,
) -> PublicationRequestEnqueue {
  let path = publication_request_path_for(instance_root: instance_root, key: key)
  let write = Filesystem.Write(
    path: path,
    content: publication_request_json(subject: subject, worktree: worktree),
  )
  if write.success {
    RequestEnqueued { path: path }
  } else {
    RequestNotEnqueued {
      detail: join([
        "the publication request could not be written, so nothing was asked of the publication helper: ",
        path, " (", write.error, ")",
      ], ""),
    }
  }
}

// THREE STATES, BECAUSE THREE REMEDIES. An answer that is ready, a request that is now waiting, and
// a spool that could not be reached are what a caller does three different things about, and an
// Optional over the receipt would collapse the last two into one silence. There was a fourth --
// a subject that could not address a spool entry -- and it is gone because publication_spool_key
// made that state unrepresentable rather than merely refused. The waiting arm in particular must NOT read as `nothing to publish`:
// that is the empty-observation narrow -- an observation that could not answer rendered as the
// verdict `nothing is affected` -- and it is what a Pending lamp looked like for the whole time
// this lane was broken.
type PublicationAnswerStanding
  = AnswerReady { receipt: PublicationReceipt }
  | AnswerAwaited { request_path: String }
  | AnswerSpoolRefused { reason: String }

// THE REQUEST IS RE-ASKED ON EVERY REACH, AND GETTING THIS WRONG WOULD HAVE FROZEN THE LAMP -- the
// same failure gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate belt_publish_gate_note records for the receipt, arriving
// one layer out through the new boundary.
//
// The tempting shape is `if an answer exists, use it; otherwise ask`, and it is wrong for exactly
// the reason the obvious idempotence key was wrong there. The belt ticks continuously, so the FIRST
// observation of a head usually happens before the worker has pushed. That observation is a
// perfectly good judgment -- branch-not-published -- and an answer kept as the standing answer would
// pin the row to `not published` forever, with every later tick re-copying the very document that
// recorded the branch's absence. The negative answer would outlive the fact it described.
//
// SO THE ASK IS UNCONDITIONAL AND THE GATE IS WHAT STOPS IT. This function is reached only after
// belt_publish_gate answers PublishProceed, and the gate proceeds only while the question is OPEN --
// a receipt saying the head is on offer halts before this is called at all. So `reached` already
// means `re-observe`, and asking again here needs no second predicate that could disagree with the
// first. The answer read is the LATEST observation the helper produced; the enqueue is what makes
// the next one exist.
//
// The read runs BEFORE the write so a reader never races its own request into a half-written
// answer. A helper mid-write from a previous tick is still possible, and it produces a decode
// refusal rather than a wrong answer -- typed, located, and gone on the next tick.
//
// A FAILED ENQUEUE REFUSES EVEN WHEN AN ANSWER WAS READ, and that ordering is deliberate rather
// than incidental. Returning the answer and swallowing the write failure would be an absorbed
// degradation: with an answer already on disk the belt would re-copy it forever and the unwritable
// request directory would never surface again, so the deficit`s frequency would be zeroed by
// construction. The answer stays perfectly good; what has failed is the obligation to re-observe,
// and that is the belt`s own problem to report.
fn publication_answer_standing(
  instance_root: String,
  subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject,
  worktree: String,
) -> PublicationAnswerStanding {
  let key = publication_spool_key(subject: subject)
  let found = publication_answer_read(
    instance_root: instance_root,
    key: key,
    expected: subject,
  )
  let asked = publication_request_enqueue(
    instance_root: instance_root,
    worktree: worktree,
    key: key,
    subject: subject,
  )
  match asked {
    RequestNotEnqueued { detail } => AnswerSpoolRefused { reason: detail }
    RequestEnqueued { path } =>
      match found {
        AnswerUnreadable { reason } => AnswerSpoolRefused { reason: reason }
        AnswerPresent { receipt } => AnswerReady { receipt: receipt }
        AnswerAbsent => AnswerAwaited { request_path: path }
      }
  }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// THE ANSWERING SIDE -- the only code in this repository that resolves a publication credential.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

// THE PROBE, MOVED HERE FROM THE BELT RATHER THAN COPIED. gunbc.roadmap_belt_actuate carried
// belt_publication_credential and its source row until this change; both are DELETED there, which
// is the deliverable rather than a tidy-up -- the belt now has no function that can name, read or
// hold a publication token, so the boundary is a property of what exists rather than of what the
// units happen to bind.
//
// WHY THE PROBE EXISTS AT ALL, unchanged from the argument it carried in the belt: a publication
// read without a usable token is not a failed read, it is a read that must not be attempted.
// Sending an empty bearer produces a 401 whose only content is that we sent nothing. Probing turns
// the most decidable failure on a fresh host into an ordinary value that becomes a recorded
// refusal, which is why an absent credential still produces an ANSWER rather than silence.
//
// THE VALUE DOES TRANSIT this function, and pretending otherwise would be the overclaim.
// shell.Env.Get returns the variable's contents, so the token is briefly a String inside this
// process; only its emptiness escapes, and nothing stores or renders it. Dissolve-on: a modeled
// presence-only environment probe, at which point this reads nothing.
//
// OutcomeIsData ADMISSIBILITY: printenv exits 1 when the variable is unset, and that exit IS the
// answer being asked for, not a fault. The result is a PublicationCredentialSource whose two arms
// ARE the adjudication, never unit.
data publication_helper_credential_env_var: NonEmptyStr = "GITHUB_TOKEN" as NonEmptyStr

// THE VARIABLE NAME IS SPELLED ONCE AND PROJECTED EVERYWHERE ELSE, including into the refusal
// prose. The first cut carried the literal four times -- the row above, this source, and both
// refusal reasons -- which is the second-representation defect this module was written to argue
// against, reproduced inside its own credential resolver. The failure it invites is quiet: renaming
// the variable in the row would leave a source reading a different name and two diagnostics naming
// a third, and every one of them would still compile.
data publication_helper_credential_source: CredentialSource = std.credentials.EnvVar { name: publication_helper_credential_env_var as String }

fn publication_helper_credential() -> PublicationCredentialSource {
  match shell.Env.Get(name: publication_helper_credential_env_var, expect: OutcomeIsData).value {
    Present { value } =>
      if trim(value) == "" {
        CredentialSourceAbsent {
          reason: join([
            publication_helper_credential_env_var as String,
            " is set to an empty value in the publication helper's environment",
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        CredentialSourceConfigured { source: publication_helper_credential_source }
      }
    Absent =>
      CredentialSourceAbsent {
        reason: join([
          publication_helper_credential_env_var as String,
          " is not set in the publication helper's environment",
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

// EVERY REQUEST GETS AN ANSWER OR A NAMED REFUSAL, and no arm is silent. A request that does not
// decode is not skipped: skipping it would leave the requester waiting forever on a document only
// the helper can see, which is a deadlock whose observable form is an ordinary Pending lamp.
//
// UNREADABLE AND REFUSED ARE TWO CLASSES WITH OPPOSITE REMEDIES, and an earlier revision carried
// only the first name for both. `HelperRequestUnreadable` is a failed READ -- a permission, a race,
// a listing that named an entry that then went away -- and it is transient: the same document may
// read cleanly on the next run, so the request stays pending and is retried. `HelperRequestRefused`
// is a failed DECODE, which is a pure function of bytes that will not change, so the judgment is
// terminal and the document leaves the pending set. Collapsing them meant the terminal class
// inherited the transient class's remedy -- retry -- which is precisely how one bad document came
// to stop the line on every tick forever. Both still stop the line; they differ in what happens to
// the request, and that is the distinction the two names carry.
type PublicationHelperOutcome
  = HelperAnswered { key: String, outcome_key: String }
  | HelperRequestNotConsumed { key: String, detail: String }
  | HelperRequestNotQuarantined { entry: String, detail: String }
  | HelperRequestUnreadable { entry: String, detail: String }
  | HelperRequestRefused { entry: String, detail: String }
  | HelperAnswerWriteFailed { key: String, detail: String }
  | HelperSubjectOutsideCapability { entry: String, detail: String }
  | HelperSpoolRefused { detail: String }

fn publication_helper_outcome_key(o: PublicationHelperOutcome) -> String {
  match o {
    HelperAnswered { key: _, outcome_key: _ } => "answered"
    HelperRequestNotConsumed { key: _, detail: _ } => "request-not-consumed"
    HelperRequestNotQuarantined { entry: _, detail: _ } => "request-not-quarantined"
    HelperRequestUnreadable { entry: _, detail: _ } => "request-unreadable"
    HelperRequestRefused { entry: _, detail: _ } => "request-refused"
    HelperAnswerWriteFailed { key: _, detail: _ } => "answer-write-failed"
    HelperSubjectOutsideCapability { entry: _, detail: _ } => "subject-outside-capability"
    HelperSpoolRefused { detail: _ } => "spool-refused"
  }
}

// WHETHER AN OUTCOME STOPS THE LINE IS TYPED, NOT A `Bool`, and the earlier revision of this
// module got that wrong. It carried `publication_helper_outcome_is_fault(o) -> Bool`, which is the
// predicate-dissolution violation this repository has already ruled on twice
// (gunbc.build_target `required_gate_decision` carries the argument): a `Bool` over a coproduct is
// a second, lossy naming of a vocabulary that already answers, and the loss is exactly what the
// caller needs. A run that learns only `true` has to walk the outcomes again to say ANYTHING about
// why it stopped.
//
// THE CONCRETE HAZARD IS THE SIXTH ARM, and it is this module's own future. A new
// PublicationHelperOutcome added to the coproduct would have to be classified in the Bool, and the
// classification a careless author reaches for is `false` -- a helper that exits 0 having failed,
// which is the fail-open this whole boundary exists to prevent. The typed disposition does not
// remove the obligation to classify, but it makes the classification carry a reason, so an arm
// added without one cannot be written as a silent non-fault.
//
// THE AUTHORITY IS NOT DELETED, only its return type corrected, for the reason the build_target
// precedent gives: WHICH outcomes stop the line is a real single authority -- an unreadable request
// and an unwritable answer must stop it, and an answer recording GitHub's refusal must not -- and
// pushing that match into each caller would let them drift.
type PublicationHelperDisposition
  = OutcomeRecorded { detail: String }
  | OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: String }

fn publication_helper_outcome_disposition(o: PublicationHelperOutcome) -> PublicationHelperDisposition {
  match o {
    HelperAnswered { key, outcome_key } =>
      OutcomeRecorded { detail: join([key, " → ", outcome_key], "") }
    HelperRequestNotConsumed { key, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([key, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperRequestUnreadable { entry, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([entry, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperRequestRefused { entry, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([entry, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperRequestNotQuarantined { entry, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([entry, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperAnswerWriteFailed { key, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([key, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperSubjectOutsideCapability { entry, detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: join([entry, ": ", detail], "") }
    HelperSpoolRefused { detail } =>
      OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: detail }
  }
}

fn publication_helper_disposition_detail(d: PublicationHelperDisposition) -> String {
  match d {
    OutcomeRecorded { detail } => detail
    OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail } => detail
  }
}

// THE STOPPING POPULATION CARRIES ITS MEMBERS, and an EMPTY stopped run has no constructor -- the
// first-plus-rest shape gunbc.fleet_posix_accounts uses for allocation conflicts, for the same
// reason: `stopped, with nothing that stopped it` is not a state. That is what lets the exit name
// what went wrong instead of dumping the whole ledger and leaving a reader to find it.
type PublicationHelperRunStanding
  = HelperRunCompleted { answered: Int }
  | HelperRunStopped {
      first: PublicationHelperOutcome
      more: List<PublicationHelperOutcome>
    }

fn publication_helper_run_standing(
  outcomes: List<PublicationHelperOutcome>,
) -> PublicationHelperRunStanding {
  fold(
    outcomes,
    init: HelperRunCompleted { answered: 0 },
    f: (acc, o) =>
      match publication_helper_outcome_disposition(o: o) {
        OutcomeRecorded { detail: _ } =>
          match acc {
            HelperRunCompleted { answered: n } => HelperRunCompleted { answered: n + 1 }
            HelperRunStopped { first, more } => HelperRunStopped { first: first, more: more }
          }
        OutcomeStopsTheLine { detail: _ } =>
          match acc {
            HelperRunCompleted { answered: _ } => HelperRunStopped { first: o, more: [] }
            HelperRunStopped { first, more } =>
              HelperRunStopped { first: first, more: concat(more, [o]) }
          }
      },
  )
}

// THE ANSWER IS WRITTEN AT AN ADDRESS THE HELPER DERIVES FROM THE SUBJECT IT JUST DECODED, never at
// one the request named. See publication_request_schema's header: a caller-supplied destination
// would be a caller-supplied write position for a principal the caller cannot otherwise reach.
// THE HELPER IS THE ONLY PRINCIPAL THAT MAY SPEND THE CREDENTIAL, so pushing the attempt branch and
// opening its pull request happen here and nowhere else. The belt has already admitted the head on
// a passed verification verdict before it enqueued the request. Only two observations may actuate:
// the branch is not on the remote, or it is there at the expected head with no pull request. Every
// other observation is answered as observed. After actuating, the helper observes AGAIN and answers
// with GitHub's own state rather than a receipt it composed from the create call, so the answer is
// never a claim the observation cannot back.
fn publication_helper_publish_title(subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject) -> NonEmptyStr {
  join(["dispatch: ", subject.node_id, " a", subject.attempt_key], "") as NonEmptyStr
}

fn publication_helper_publish_body(subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject) -> String {
  join([
    "Opened by the gunbc roadmap belt for node ", subject.node_id, ", attempt ", subject.attempt_key,
    ", after verification passed for head ", subject.expected_head as String, ".",
  ], "")
}

fn publication_helper_publish_if_unpublished(
  credential: PublicationCredentialSource,
  subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject,
  worktree: String,
  observed: PublicationOutcome,
) -> PublicationOutcome {
  let unpublished = match observed {
    BranchNotPublished { branch: _, remote_branches_seen: _ } => true
    RemoteBranchAtExpectedHeadWithoutPullRequest { branch: _, head_sha: _ } => true
    PublicationBound { branch: _, pr_number: _, pr_head: _, url: _, draft: _ } => false
    PublicationHeadDiverged { branch: _, pr_number: _, pr_head: _, expected_head: _, url: _ } => false
    PublicationBindingAmbiguous { branch: _, base_ref: _, pr_numbers: _ } => false
    RemoteBranchHeadDiverged { branch: _, remote_head: _, expected_head: _ } => false
    RemoteBranchBindingAmbiguous { branch: _, remote: _, refs_matched: _ } => false
    PublicationObservationRefused { detail: _ } => false
  }
  if !unpublished {
    observed
  } else {
    match credential {
      CredentialSourceAbsent { reason } =>
        PublicationObservationRefused { detail: join(["the branch is unpublished and no credential is configured to publish it: ", reason], "") }
      CredentialSourceConfigured { source } =>
        match publish_attempt(
          repo: worktree as FilePath,
          remote: publication_subject_remote(subject: subject),
          branch: subject.branch,
          base: subject.expected_base,
          owner: subject.repository.owner,
          repo_name: subject.repository.name,
          title: publication_helper_publish_title(subject: subject),
          body: publication_helper_publish_body(subject: subject),
          credential: source,
        ) {
          PushRefused { exit_code, stderr } =>
            PublicationObservationRefused { detail: join(["the attempt branch could not be pushed (exit ", to_string(value: exit_code), "): ", stderr], "") }
          PullRequestRefused { detail } =>
            PublicationObservationRefused { detail: join(["the pull request could not be opened: ", detail], "") }
          PublishActuated { pr_number: _, url: _ } =>
            observe_publication_for_subject(credential: credential, subject: subject)
        }
    }
  }
}

// STOPPING THE LINE AND CONSUMING THE REQUEST ARE TWO DECISIONS, AND THE EARLIER REVISION MADE ONE.
//
// Every other terminal outcome consumes its request -- the answer is written, then the request is
// deleted -- so the next run sees a spool holding only open questions. An UNREADABLE request was the
// exception: the line stopped and the document stayed, so the same bytes were re-read, re-refused
// and re-charged a full run every tick, forever, with every request behind it waiting. The refusal
// was right; the retention was the defect. Measured on srv1 (2026-09-06): two requests written under
// a superseded request schema stopped the helper on every tick for a day at ~2 CPU-minutes each,
// and no run in that population could have ended differently, because decoding is a pure function
// of the bytes.
//
// So the document moves out of the pending set and the line still stops. The stop is what makes the
// deficit loud and analyzable; the move is what makes it stop ONCE per bad document rather than once
// per tick. A NEW undecodable request stops the line again, which is the intended signal -- what is
// removed is only the repetition, never the refusal.
//
// A MOVE THAT FAILS IS ITS OWN OUTCOME, on the precedent the delete-failure arm above sets. Silence
// there would put the helper back in exactly the loop this function exists to end, with nothing
// recording that it had tried to leave it.
// WHETHER A REQUEST IS TERMINALLY REFUSED IS A PURE JUDGMENT, AND RETIRING IT IS AN EFFECT.
//
// Separating them is not tidiness. Every one of these refusals is decidable from the entry name and
// the request bytes alone, so the judgment can be witnessed hermetically -- and an earlier revision
// of this repair fused them, which put a filesystem move on the decision path and turned two
// existing witnesses into route gaps: the hermetic route has no arm for the move, so the claims
// stopped before reaching the subject they were written to assert. The available repair was to mock
// the move, which would have passed them against a fabricated success. The judgment moved instead.
type RequestAdmission
  = RequestAdmitted { subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject, worktree: String }
  | RequestUndecodableDocument { reason: String }
  | RequestOutsideCapability { detail: String }
  | RequestMisaddressed { detail: String }

// THE THREE REFUSALS STAY THREE, rather than collapsing into one `terminally refused`. They share a
// remedy -- leave the pending set -- and share nothing else: a document that does not parse, a
// document that names a repository this principal may not act on, and a document whose name lies
// about its own subject are three different things for whoever reads the stopped line, and the
// second is the anti-forgery arm. One name for all three would be the state-space conflation this
// module already avoids in its outcome vocabulary.
fn publication_request_admission(entry: String, request_text: String) -> RequestAdmission {
  match publication_request_decode(request: request_text) {
    RequestUndecodable { reason } => RequestUndecodableDocument { reason: reason }
    RequestDecoded { subject, worktree } =>
      match publication_helper_capability_admits(subject: subject) {
        SubjectOutsideCapability { detail } => RequestOutsideCapability { detail: detail }
        SubjectWithinCapability =>
          if publication_spool_entry_name(key: publication_spool_key(subject: subject)) != entry {
            RequestMisaddressed {
              detail: join([
                "the request's own subject addresses ",
                publication_spool_entry_name(key: publication_spool_key(subject: subject)),
                ", so the document at this entry is not about the judgment its name claims",
              ], ""),
            }
          } else {
            RequestAdmitted { subject: subject, worktree: worktree }
          }
      }
  }
}

// A MOVE THAT FAILS IS ITS OWN OUTCOME, on the precedent the delete-failure arm below sets. Silence
// there would put the helper back in exactly the loop this function exists to end, with nothing
// recording that it had tried to leave it. The refusal the caller already decided is returned
// unchanged on success, so retiring a request never renames why it was refused.
fn publication_helper_retire_request(
  instance_root: String,
  entry: String,
  refused: PublicationHelperOutcome,
) -> PublicationHelperOutcome {
  let moved = shell.Move.File(
    source: join([publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry], "") as FilePath,
    destination: join([publication_refused_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry], "") as FilePath,
  )
  if moved.success {
    refused
  } else {
    HelperRequestNotQuarantined {
      entry: entry,
      detail: join([
        "the request was terminally refused and could not be moved out of the pending set, so it will stop the line again on the next run with the same bytes: ",
        publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry,
        " -> ", publication_refused_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry,
      ], ""),
    }
  }
}

fn publication_helper_answer_request(
  instance_root: String,
  credential: PublicationCredentialSource,
  entry: String,
  request_text: String,
) -> PublicationHelperOutcome {
  match publication_request_admission(entry: entry, request_text: request_text) {
    RequestUndecodableDocument { reason } =>
      publication_helper_retire_request(
        instance_root: instance_root,
        entry: entry,
        refused: HelperRequestRefused { entry: entry, detail: reason },
      )
    RequestOutsideCapability { detail } =>
      publication_helper_retire_request(
        instance_root: instance_root,
        entry: entry,
        refused: HelperSubjectOutsideCapability { entry: entry, detail: detail },
      )
    RequestMisaddressed { detail } =>
      publication_helper_retire_request(
        instance_root: instance_root,
        entry: entry,
        refused: HelperRequestRefused { entry: entry, detail: detail },
      )
    RequestAdmitted { subject, worktree } => {
      let key = publication_spool_key(subject: subject)
      let receipt = publication_receipt_of(
              subject: subject,
              outcome: publication_helper_publish_if_unpublished(
                credential: credential,
                subject: subject,
                worktree: worktree,
                observed: observe_publication_for_subject(
                  credential: credential,
                  subject: subject,
                ),
              ),
            )
            let write = Filesystem.Write(
              path: publication_answer_path_for(instance_root: instance_root, key: key),
              content: publication_receipt_json(receipt: receipt),
            )
            if !write.success {
              HelperAnswerWriteFailed {
                key: key,
                detail: join([
                  "the publication answer could not be written: ", write.error,
                ], ""),
              }
            } else {
              let consumed = Filesystem.Delete(
                path: join([
                  publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry,
                ], ""),
              )
              if consumed.success {
                HelperAnswered {
                  key: key,
                  outcome_key: publication_receipt_outcome_key(outcome: receipt.outcome) as String,
                }
              } else {
                HelperRequestNotConsumed {
                  key: key,
                  detail: join([
                    "the answer was written and its request could not be removed, so this subject would be re-observed every run with nothing recording why: ",
                    consumed.error,
                  ], ""),
                }
              }
            }
    }
  }
}

// A CREDENTIAL BOUNDARY WITHOUT A SUBJECT BOUNDARY RELOCATES THE AUTHORITY INSTEAD OF CONFINING IT,
// and this function is the half the first cut of this module was missing.
//
// The principal boundary answers WHO holds the credential. It says nothing about WHAT the credential
// may be spent on, and RoadmapPublicationSubject carries its own `repository` field. So the service
// principal -- which cannot read the token -- could hand the helper a request naming ANY repository
// and have the helper spend the credential against it on the requester`s behalf. That is a confused
// deputy in its textbook form: the helper has an authority the caller lacks, and was taking the
// caller`s word for the target it should be used on.
//
// THE TOKEN`S NARROW SCOPE BOUNDS THE DAMAGE AND DOES NOT CLOSE THE HOLE. The publication capability
// is Pull requests: read over one repository, so today the worst case is reading public PR state
// somewhere else. That is an argument about the current token, not about this code, and it expires
// silently the moment a wider credential is placed at the same path -- at which point an unguarded
// path is already there and nobody re-derives this reasoning. The refusal belongs here, where the
// capability is a modeled fact, rather than in an assumption about what the operator installed.
//
// SO THE CHECK RUNS BEFORE ANY NETWORK ACCESS and compares the request`s repository against
// gunbc.repository gunbc_repository, the single authority for which repository this project is. It
// refuses rather than narrowing: a request outside the capability is a defect or an attempt, and
// both want the line stopped and the document left in place for analysis rather than a quiet
// substitution of the permitted repository for the requested one -- which would answer a question
// nobody asked and record it as though they had.
type PublicationCapabilityAdmission
  = SubjectWithinCapability
  | SubjectOutsideCapability { detail: String }

fn publication_helper_capability_admits(
  subject: RoadmapPublicationSubject,
) -> PublicationCapabilityAdmission {
  if subject.repository.owner != gunbc_repository.owner {
    SubjectOutsideCapability {
      detail: join([
        "the request names repository owner ", subject.repository.owner,
        ", and the publication capability covers only ", gunbc_repository.owner,
        "/", gunbc_repository.name,
        " -- the credential is not spent on a target the requester chose",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else if subject.repository.name != gunbc_repository.name {
    SubjectOutsideCapability {
      detail: join([
        "the request names repository ", subject.repository.owner, "/", subject.repository.name,
        ", and the publication capability covers only ", gunbc_repository.owner,
        "/", gunbc_repository.name,
        " -- the credential is not spent on a target the requester chose",
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    SubjectWithinCapability
  }
}

// A REQUEST'S PRESENCE IS THE ASK, AND THE ANSWER IS NOT A CACHE.
//
// The helper does not skip an entry because an answer already exists for it. Publication is an
// observation of a remote that has not stopped moving, so a standing answer is a snapshot rather
// than a settled fact -- and a helper that treated it as settled would pin every row whose first
// observation preceded the worker's push, which is the freeze publication_answer_standing's header
// describes from the requesting side. The requester enqueues only while its own gate says the
// question is open, so a request that exists is a question that is still open, and the only correct
// response to it is to observe.
//
// THE REQUEST IS CONSUMED BY THE ANSWER, and the ordering is write-then-delete. A delete that
// preceded the write would lose the question on any failure after it; this way the worst case is a
// request answered twice, which costs one network read and produces identical bytes. A delete that
// FAILS is its own outcome rather than a silence, because the alternative is a subject re-observed
// on every run forever with nothing saying why.
fn publication_helper_entry_outcome(
  instance_root: String,
  credential: PublicationCredentialSource,
  entry: String,
) -> PublicationHelperOutcome {
  if trim(entry) == "" {
    HelperSpoolRefused { detail: "the request listing carried an empty entry name" }
  } else {
    let path = join([
      publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root), "/", entry,
    ], "")
    let read = Filesystem.Read(path: path)
    if !read.success {
      HelperRequestUnreadable {
        entry: entry,
        detail: join([
          "a publication request was listed and then could not be read: ",
          path, " (", read.error, ")",
        ], ""),
      }
    } else {
      publication_helper_answer_request(
        instance_root: instance_root,
        credential: credential,
        entry: entry,
        request_text: read.content,
      )
    }
  }
}

// ONE CREDENTIAL PROBE PER RUN, not one per request, for the reason belt_publish_pass_note gives
// for the probe it replaces: the environment does not change between requests within a run, and
// probing per request would multiply a process spawn by the request count for an answer that
// cannot differ.
//
// AN UNLISTABLE REQUEST SPOOL REFUSES AND DOES NOT WIDEN. There is no arm that answers `assume
// there is nothing to do` -- that is the empty-observation narrow, and it would render a helper
// whose spool the deploy never created as a helper with no work, forever.
fn publication_helper_run_once(instance_root: String) -> List<PublicationHelperOutcome> {
  let requests_dir = publication_request_dir(instance_root: instance_root)
  let requests = Filesystem.List(path: requests_dir)
  if !requests.success {
    [
      HelperSpoolRefused {
        detail: join([
          "the publication request directory could not be listed, so it is unknown whether anything was asked: ",
          requests_dir, " (", requests.error, ")",
        ], ""),
      },
    ]
  } else {
    let credential = publication_helper_credential()
    map(
      filter(xs: split(s: requests.entries, delimiter: "\n"), f: e => trim(e) != ""),
      entry => publication_helper_entry_outcome(
        instance_root: instance_root,
        credential: credential,
        entry: trim(entry),
      ),
    )
  }
}

fn publication_helper_run_json(outcomes: List<PublicationHelperOutcome>) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: "roadmap-publication-helper-run/v1")),
    json_kv(
      key: "outcomes",
      value: json_array(elements: map(outcomes, o => json_object(members: [
        json_kv(key: "outcome", value: json_string(s: publication_helper_outcome_key(o: o))),
        json_kv(
          key: "detail",
          value: json_string(s: publication_helper_disposition_detail(
            d: publication_helper_outcome_disposition(o: o),
          )),
        ),
      ]))),
    ),
  ]))
}

// EXIT SEMANTICS, on the belt's own precedent (belt_run_once_cli_note): a recorded refusal is a
// counted outcome and not a failure to run, so an answer recording an absent credential exits 0 --
// the fact reached disk and the requester will read it. Nonzero is reserved for the helper failing
// to do its job at all: a spool it cannot reach, a request it cannot read, an answer it cannot
// write. That is what lets systemd distinguish `the helper is broken` from `the helper ran and
// GitHub said no`.
fn publication_helper_run_once_cli_in(instance_root: String) -> ProcessExit {
  let outcomes = publication_helper_run_once(instance_root: instance_root)
  match publication_helper_run_standing(outcomes: outcomes) {
    HelperRunCompleted { answered: _ } => ExitSuccess
    HelperRunStopped { first: _, more: _ } =>
      exit_failure(reason: publication_helper_run_json(outcomes: outcomes))
  }
}

// THE INSTANCE ROOT IS A REQUIRED ARGUMENT AND HAS NO DEFAULT, for the reason
// gunbc.live_deploy.spec deployment_owned_steps carries, after the belt's own workdir was a literal projection of
// production's tree: a helper that defaults would answer requests for whichever instance the
// default names, whatever instance installed it, and two slots on one host would fuse their spools
// silently. An empty argument REFUSES rather than falling back.
fn publication_helper_run_once_cli(instance_root: String) -> ProcessExit {
  if trim(instance_root) == "" {
    exit_failure(reason: "publication_helper_run_once_cli: instance_root is empty — the helper has no spool to answer from (fail-closed)")
  } else {
    publication_helper_run_once_cli_in(instance_root: trim(instance_root))
  }
}
